# Frontend Architecture

## Stack

- **Workbench**: `projectos/workbench` — Vite + React 18 + TypeScript
- **State**: Zustand (`src/state/workbenchStore.ts`)
- **Styling**: CSS Modules + global tokens (`src/styles/tokens.css`)
- **Icons**: `lucide-react` only — no ad-hoc SVGs in feature code
- **Contracts**: shared types in `projectos/contracts/` — UI must not redefine API shapes

## Directory Layout

```
workbench/src/
├── components/     # Feature + shared UI (ui/ = design-system primitives)
├── layout/         # App shell, panels, zone layout
├── state/          # Zustand store
├── styles/         # tokens.css, motion.css, global.css
└── i18n/           # en/zh strings — no hardcoded user-facing copy
```

## Layer Rules

1. **`components/ui/`** — reusable primitives (Button, Chip, TabBar). No business logic or API calls.
2. **Feature components** — compose `ui/` primitives; read/write via store or props only.
3. **`layout/`** — structural chrome (AppShell, ThreeZoneLayout). Apply frost glass (Tier A) here first.
4. **`components/Glass/`** (when added) — Tier B true lens (`GlassScene`, `GlassLens`). Hero overlays only.
5. **Contracts** — import from `projectos/contracts`; never duplicate type definitions in workbench.

## Styling Conventions

- One `*.module.css` per component, colocated with its `.tsx`.
- Use CSS variables from `tokens.css` — never hardcode colors/spacing in modules.
- Glass on **navigation chrome only** — never on chat cards, lists, graph nodes (see `liquid-glass-ui.mdc`).
- **Two tiers:** Tier A = `backdrop-filter` frost for bulk nav; Tier B = `GlassLens` SVG refraction for palette/dropdowns (see `apple-inspired-ui` skill).
- New surfaces: start from `.cursor/skills/apple-inspired-ui/components/` recipes.

## Data Flow

```
contracts → client (Mock/Real) → workbenchStore → components
```

Mock fixtures live in `projectos/client/fixtures/`. UI code must work in both mock and real modes.

## Do Not

- Add CSS-in-JS or Tailwind — stay on CSS Modules + tokens.
- Put fetch/API logic inside presentational components.
- Create one-off panel styles — extend glass surface patterns instead.
